a	2026-02-22-23-Leveraging-Attributes-With-Respect-Validation-and-Laravel2026-02-22-23-Aproveitando-Atributos-com-Respect-Validation-e-Laravel.pt
t	Leveraging Attributes With Respect\Validation and Laravel
i	Alexandre Gomes Gaigalas – February 22, 2026
p	xThe full example for this blog post is kavailable on GitHubd (https://github.com/alganet/attributes-laravel-validation)x.
h	Context
p	xValidation logic often ends up distributed across request rules, controller checks, model helpers, and serializers. In that arrangement, constraints for the same field can diverge over time.
p	xThis example uses a single domain object as the source of truth for constraints and payload mapping, then integrates that object into Laravel request handling and persistence.
h	Design Objective
p	xThe objective is to keep one authoritative definition for:
b	xdomain constraints
b	xvalidation messages
b	xpersistence/output field mapping
p	mPitchDraftx is that authoritative definition.
h	Domain Contract
p	mPitchDraftx declares constraints directly on typed constructor parameters with Respect attributes.
c	Kfinal. Kreadonly. Kclass. LPitchDraft
c	.{
c	.    Kpublic. Kfunction. F__construct.(
c	.        #[LNamed.('Speaker Name', Knew. LAllOf.(Knew. LStringType., Knew. LLength.(Knew. LBetween.(4, 60))))]
c	.        Kpublic. Kstring. .$speaker_name.,
c	
c	.        #[LNamed.('Speaker Email', Knew. LEmail.)]
c	.        Kpublic. Kstring. .$speaker_email.,
c	
c	.        #[LNamed.('Talk Title', Knew. LShortCircuit.(Knew. LLength.(Knew. LBetween.(12, 90)), Knew. LContains.('Laravel')))]
c	.        Kpublic. Kstring. .$talk_title.,
c	
c	.        #[LNamed.('Talk Duration', Knew. LAllOf.(Knew. LIntVal., Knew. LBetween.(20, 45)))]
c	.        Kpublic. Kint. .$talk_duration_minutes.,
c	
c	.        #[LNamed.('Skill Level', Knew. LTemplated.('Skill level must be either {{haystack|list:or}}.', Knew. LIn.([S'intermediate'., S'advanced'.]))) ]
c	.        Kpublic. Kstring. .$skill_level.,
c	.    ) {}
c	.}
c	
p	xThis keeps rule composition explicit (mAllOfx, mShortCircuitx, mBetweenx, mEachx) and localizes message customization (mNamedx, mTemplatedx).
h	Request Boundary
p	xForm requests handle transport sanitation and coercion, then construct the DTO.
c	Kpublic. Kfunction. FtoPitchDraft.(): LPitchDraft
c	.{
c	.    .$sanitized. = $this->Fvalidated.();
c	
c	.    Kreturn. Knew. LPitchDraft.(
c	.        speaker_name: Ftrim.(K(string.) ($sanitized['speaker_name'] ?? '')),
c	.        speaker_email: Ftrim.(K(string.) ($sanitized['speaker_email'] ?? '')),
c	.        talk_title: Ftrim.(K(string.) ($sanitized['talk_title'] ?? '')),
c	.        talk_duration_minutes: $this->FnormalizeDuration.($sanitized['talk_duration_minutes'] ?? Knull.),
c	.        skill_level: Ftrim.(K(string.) ($sanitized['skill_level'] ?? '')),
c	.        highlights: Farray_values.(Farray_filter.(Farray_map.('trim', $sanitized['highlights'] ?? []))),
c	.    );
c	.}
c	
p	xRequest attributes remain focused on HTTP behavior, like m#[RedirectToRoute]x.
h	Adapter to Laravel Exceptions
p	xRespect\Validation exceptions are converted to Laravel mValidationExceptionx once, in a small adapter.
c	Kfinal. Kclass. LRespectAttributeValidator
c	.{
c	.    Kpublic. Kfunction. Fvalidate.(Kobject. $dto): Kvoid
c	.    {
c	.        Ktry. {
c	.            Lv.::Fattributes.()->Fassert.($dto);
c	.        } Kcatch. (LRespectValidationException. $exception) {
c	.            $messages = $exception->FgetMessages.();
c	.            Funset.($messages['__root__']);
c	
c	.            Kthrow. LValidationException.::FwithMessages.($messages);
c	.        }
c	.    }
c	.}
c	
p	xThis preserves standard Laravel response behavior for both web and API flows. If your project has multiple DTO classes, you can use this same adapter for them all.
h	Reusing One Payload Map
p	mPitchDraft::toAttributes()x is reused for both persistence and API output.
b	xpersistence path: mPitch::create($draft->toAttributes())
b	xresponse path: m$pitch->toDraft()->toAttributes()
p	xUsing one mapping function avoids duplicated field lists and keeps write/read payloads aligned.
h	Operational Notes
p	xThe approach introduces one integration component (mRespectAttributeValidatorx) and one DTO-to-model reconstruction method (mPitch::toDraft()x). In exchange, the validation contract is explicit, centralized, and reusable across multiple entry points.
h	Summary
p	xThis architecture keeps Laravel responsible for transport and persistence concerns, while domain validation remains defined in one DTO contract through Respect attributes. The result is lower duplication and a stricter single source of truth for validation logic.
